          "content": "CIRCLES"
        },
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.76,
            0.258,
            0.885,
            0.328
          ],
          "content": "10"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.097,
            0.376,
            0.292,
            0.396
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "10.1 Introduction"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.095,
            0.406,
            0.898,
            0.505
          ],
          "content": "You have studied in Class IX that a circle is a collection of all points in a plane which are at a constant distance (radius) from a fixed point (centre). You have also studied various terms related to a circle like chord, segment, sector, arc etc. Let us now examine the different situations that can arise when a circle and a line are given in a plane."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.095,
            0.508,
            0.898,
            0.547
          ],
          "content": "So, let us consider a circle and a line PQ. There can be three possibilities given in Fig. 10.1 below: 1"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.551,
            0.369,
            0.738
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.235,
                0.741,
                0.262,
                0.76
              ],
              "content": "(i)"
            }
          ],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_0_image_7.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.378,
            0.536,
            0.616,
            0.741
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.489,
                0.742,
                0.521,
                0.759
              ],
              "content": "(ii)"
            }
          ],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_0_image_9.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.642,
            0.551,
            0.885,
            0.735
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.759,
                0.742,
                0.795,
                0.759
              ],
              "content": "(iii)"
            },
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.448,
                0.767,
                0.545,
                0.787
              ],
              "content": "Fig. 10.1"
            }
          ],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_0_image_11.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.094,
            0.797,
            0.901,
            0.913
          ],
          "content": "In Fig. 10.1 (i), the line PQ and the circle have no common point. In this case, PQ is called a **non-intersecting** line with respect to the circle. In Fig. 10.1 (ii), there are two common points A and B that the line PQ and the circle have. In this case, we call the line PQ a **secant** of the circle. In Fig. 10.1 (iii), there is only one point A which is common to the line PQ and the circle. In this case, the line is called a **tangent** to the circle."
        },
        {
